Creates an assumption instance for a two-dimensional <code>int</code> array.

Examples:

Executed test:
<pre><code class='java'>
{@literal @Test}
public void given_the_assumption_is_met_the_test_is_executed() {
    given(new int[][] {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}).contains(new int[] {1, 2}, atIndex(0));
    // the remaining code is executed
    // ...
}
</code></pre>

Skipped test:
<pre><code class='java'>
{@literal @Test}
public void given_the_assumption_is_not_met_the_test_is_skipped() {
    given(new int[][] {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}).contains(new int[] {1, 2}, atIndex(1));
    // the remaining code is NOT executed
    // ...
}
</code></pre>

Use a custom comparator instead of relying on the type's equals method for assertion checks. The custom comparator is bound to the assertion instance; if a new assertion instance is created, the default comparison strategy will be used instead.

Example:
assertThat(frodo).usingComparator(raceComparator, "Hobbit Race Comparator").isEqualTo(sam);

Create a {@link FilterOperator} to use in {@link AbstractIterableAssert#filteredOn(String, FilterOperator) filteredOn(String, FilterOperator)} to express a filter keeping all Iterable elements whose property/field value does not match any of the given values. <p>As often, an example helps:<pre><code class='java'>
Employee yoda   = new Employee(1L, new Name("Yoda"), 800);
Employee obiwan = new Employee(2L, new Name("Obiwan"), 800);
Employee luke   = new Employee(3L, new Name("Luke", "Skywalker"), 26);
Employee noname = new Employee(4L, null, 50);

Creates an assumption instance for an {@link AtomicLong} value. Examples show how the test executes when the assumption is met and is skipped when not met.

Executed test:
<pre><code class='java'>
{@literal @Test}
public void given_the_assumption_is_met_the_test_is_executed() {
  given(new AtomicLong(1L)).hasNonNegativeValue();
  // the remaining code is executed
}
</code></pre>

Skipped test:
<pre><code class='java'>
{@literal @Test}
public void given_the_assumption_is_not_met_the_test_is_skipped() {
  given(new AtomicLong(1L)).hasNegativeValue();
  // the remaining code is NOT executed
}
</code></pre>

